<h2>Applying the Categorical Imperative</h2>
<p><em>Testing Kant on a concrete case of your choosing</em></p>
<p>Kant gives us a remarkable tool: a procedure for evaluating any proposed action by asking whether we could will the principle behind it as a universal law, or whether the action would treat someone merely as a means. The tool is only as good as its application, though. This reflection asks you to try the tool on a case you actually care about.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>The prompt</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step one: Pick a formulation of the categorical imperative.</strong> Use either the <em>Universalization</em> formulation ("Act only on that maxim by which you can at the same time will that it should become a universal law") or the <em>Humanity</em> formulation ("Treat humanity, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, never merely as a means, but always at the same time as an end"). You only need to pick one.</p>
<p><strong>Step two: Pick a concrete action or case.</strong> Something specific enough to analyze. Examples: lying to a sick friend to spare them bad news, breaking a promise to attend a family event to help a stranger, downloading copyrighted content for personal use, refusing to lend money to a relative, buying goods known to involve exploitative labor. The case can come from your own life, from current events, or from a scenario you construct. It must be specific, not generic.</p>
<p><strong>Step three: In your reflection, do the following:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>State the action clearly and state the maxim behind it in general terms.</li>
<li>Apply your chosen formulation step by step. If you chose the Universalization formulation, imagine the maxim as a universal law and explain whether and why it produces a contradiction. If you chose the Humanity formulation, identify whether and how the action treats someone merely as a means.</li>
<li>State what Kant's framework would conclude about the action.</li>
<li>Raise and briefly address one challenge to your conclusion. Does the case depend on how the maxim is stated? Is there a way of formulating the maxim that would produce a different result? Would Kant's framework rule out an action that seems morally permissible, or permit one that seems wrong?</li>
</ol>
</div></div>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8505;&#65039;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>On stating the maxim</strong></p>
<p>The most common mistake in applying the Universalization test is stating the maxim too narrowly or too broadly. Too narrow: "I will tell a lie about being sick on November 4 to get out of this particular exam." This is a description of one action, not a maxim. Too broad: "I will pursue my own interests." This is not really a maxim either; it is a statement of self-interest in general. The maxim should state the principle that actually guides the action, at a level general enough to be a principle but specific enough to be evaluated. "I will lie when telling the truth would cause me discomfort" is a usable maxim. Think carefully about how to state your maxim before you apply the test.</p>
</div></div>
